capability number/float-bits  a float's IEEE 754 bits as an unsigned integer, and the float a pattern of bits holds
  heroes  numbers.f64_to_bits, numbers.bits_to_f64, numbers.f32_to_bits, numbers.bits_to_f32
  go      math.Float64bits, math.Float64frombits, math.Float32bits, math.Float32frombits
  rust    f64::to_bits, f64::from_bits, f32::to_bits, f32::from_bits
  python  struct.pack, struct.unpack (formats `d` and `f`)
  note    added 2026-11-18 (repair queue 74), so `protobuf` and `sql/mysql` share one and a MySQL program no longer carries `protobuf` for it. Exact for every float but a NaN, both zeros' signs, the subnormals and the infinities included: every exponent's first, middle and last pattern of both widths and both signs goes to its float and back unchanged, and the patterns tested are Python's `struct.pack`'s. Every NaN gives the quiet NaN with its sign clear, where Go and Rust keep a NaN's sign and payload: the language reads neither (no arithmetic shows them, `to_str` writes every NaN as `nan`), and C's `memcpy` cannot be bound for it, since its `void *` takes only bytes on both sides and `.ptr()` lends only a field of bytes (the compiler's `ffi_parameter_type` and `bad_operand`, run 2026-11-18). Keeping them needs a door of the language, a built-in or a function of its runtime

capability number/half-float  a float rounded to IEEE 754's binary16, the half-precision float, as its 16 bits, and those bits read back as the value they hold
  heroes  numbers.f64_to_f16_bits, numbers.f32_to_f16_bits, numbers.f16_bits_to_f64, numbers.f16_bits_to_f32
  go      -
  rust    f16::from_bits, f16::to_bits, f16::from (into f32 and f64), `as f16` from f32 and f64 (the primitive unstable in Rust 1.100)
  python  struct.pack, struct.unpack, struct.calcsize (format `e`)
  note    added 2026-11-17 (queue 58), the yardstick Rust's crate half (f16::from_f32, f16::from_f64, f16::to_f32, f16::to_f64, f16::from_bits, f16::to_bits); rounded half to even from the exact value, as IEEE 754 asks and clang's `_Float16` rounds: 35 values against Python's struct and clang 21 on 2026-11-17 and every one of the 65536 halves read and written back. A value of 65520 or more is an infinity of its sign, as IEEE 754, clang and half answer, where Python's struct raises OverflowError. Every NaN becomes the quiet NaN 0x7e00, its sign and payload lost, since the language reads neither; Python and clang keep the sign (-nan is 0xfe00)


capability number/byte-size  a count of bytes written for people, `1.2 MB` or `1.2 MiB`, and read back
  heroes  numbers.format_size, numbers.parse_size, numbers.SizeUnits
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    added 2026-11-17, from the community census; none of the three standard libraries writes one (go-humanize's Bytes and IBytes and Python's humanize.naturalsize are the libraries people reach for). The caller chooses the powers of 1000 (`kB` as the SI writes the kilo) or of 1024 (`KiB`) and the digits after the point; the quotient's digits are exact and the last rounded half to even, every expected value observed with Python's `decimal` at 80 digits; a quotient that rounds to a whole unit more is written in that unit, `1.0 MB` for 999,950 bytes and not `1000.0 kB` (a decision; humanize, not on this machine, was not asked). Reading takes either case, a space or none, `B` or none, and the `i` units always as 1024s; the caller says whether `KB` is 1000 or 1024, which JEDEC and Windows read as 1024; the product is exact on `numbers`' own natural numbers and rounded half to even, past an `i64` `does_not_fit`

dropped
  go      strconv.AppendBool, strconv.AppendFloat, strconv.AppendInt, strconv.AppendUint, strconv.AppendQuote, strconv.AppendQuoteRune, strconv.AppendQuoteRuneToASCII, strconv.AppendQuoteRuneToGraphic, strconv.AppendQuoteToASCII, strconv.AppendQuoteToGraphic: the same texts appended to a caller's buffer, a capacity hint; a program collects pieces and joins once
  go      strconv.IntSize: the width of Go's `int`, which no Heroes type has, every width being in its type's name
  rust    i64::from_ascii_bytes, i64::from_ascii_bytes_radix and the same of every width: the parse of bytes not yet checked as text; `validated_bytes` makes them text and the parse reads it
  rust    i64::format_into, fmt::NumBuffer: a caller's buffer to write the digits into, a capacity hint
  rust    FromStr and the formatting traits of i128, u128, isize, usize, num::NonZero, num::Wrapping, num::Saturating: types the language does not have
  rust    f16's arithmetic, comparisons and functions (abs, sqrt, powi, total_cmp, next_up and the rest of its methods): a type the language does not have; a half is held as its bits and computed on as an `f32` or an `f64`, which hold every half exactly
  rust    fmt::Formatter, fmt::FormattingOptions, fmt::Alignment, fmt::Sign, fmt::DebugAsHex: the machinery a trait's `fmt` is handed, where Heroes has no traits; what they select is in the blocks above
  python  the operator methods of int, float and bool (`__add__`, `__sub__`, `__mul__`, `__truediv__`, `__floordiv__`, `__mod__`, `__divmod__`, `__pow__`, `__neg__`, `__pos__`, `__abs__`, `__invert__`, `__and__`, `__or__`, `__xor__`, `__lshift__`, `__rshift__`, their reflected forms `__radd__`, `__rsub__`, `__rmul__`, `__rtruediv__`, `__rfloordiv__`, `__rmod__`, `__rdivmod__`, `__rpow__`, `__rand__`, `__ror__`, `__rxor__`, `__rlshift__`, `__rrshift__`, `__eq__`, `__ne__`, `__lt__`, `__le__`, `__gt__`, `__ge__`): the language's own operators
  python  the object protocol of int, float and bool (`__class__`, `__delattr__`, `__dir__`, `__doc__`, `__getattribute__`, `__getnewargs__`, `__getstate__`, `__hash__`, `__index__`, `__init__`, `__init_subclass__`, `__reduce__`, `__reduce_ex__`, `__setattr__`, `__sizeof__`, `__subclasshook__`, `__getformat__`): what every Python object answers, nothing about numbers
  python  int.conjugate, int.real, int.imag, int.numerator, int.denominator, float.conjugate, float.real, float.imag: the numeric tower's identities, an integer being its own numerator, real part and conjugate
